      "latin": "jūglāns, jūglandis f.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "walnut; also the walnut tree",
      "evidence": [
        "Varro, LL 5.102",
        "Pliny, NH 15.86, 15.91",
        "Traupman",
        "Traupman 73, 403"
      ],
      "comment": "Lewis &amp; Short: “a walnut; a walnut-tree”. The long ā in the nominative follows Traupman; it is not carried into the oblique stem. Varro, LL 5.102 derives the name from Jupiter’s acorn: <span lang=\"la\">ā Jove et glande jūglāns est appellāta</span>. Pliny, NH 15.86 and 15.91: <strong lang=\"la\">nucēs jūglandēs</strong>, with the green husk (<strong lang=\"la\">calyx, calycis m.</strong>) and the woody shell (<strong lang=\"la\">putāmen, putāminis n.</strong>)."
    }
  ],
